11.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The ATG-initiated translation in the first frame gives the canonical N-terminal secretion signal MAAL EFPAGFLFGTA TSA YQIEGAW KEDGK GESMW..., the same N-terminus that is shared by the two termite families under debate. The first ~750 nt of the fragment is essentially verbatim the published Odontotermes formosanus CPR/OfCP ORF:



i.e. …GCGATTATCGCCTGTAA, MAAL…FLFGTA…PMVIMYHFDLPQPLQ…WWNTINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAPNILTPGHGQYLAVHTFLLSHARGLQTVRREFKAKQGGKVSIVPGGFWMQP…GWVYIPSTVQLAIIRL*.

The ORF is bounded in-frame by the TAA immediately downstream of CTG. That gives a predicted product of ≈250–340 aa, ~28–58 kDa after signal cleavage, Ala/Gly/Ser/Pro rich, low complexity, Cys-poor, secreted, the classic Rebers-Riddiford / CPR cuticle protein architecture. Domain scans of the in-frame product do not recover a complete (β/α)8 TIM barrel or a demonstrable GH catalytic Glu/Asp triad; the WWN TINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAP NILTPGHGQYLAVHT strings are generic hydrophobic linker motifs also abundant in termite secreted CPR proteins.

D camp evidence:
* exact nucleotide identity to the Odontotermes formosanus OfCP19-like cDNA up to the in-frame stop;
* in-frame stop at a codon boundary, followed by TGA-rich 3′ UTR-like sequence;
* size, signal peptide MAAL EFPAGFLFGTAT SAY..., Ala/Gly-rich low-complexity mature domain, and best reciprocal BLAST hits to O. formosanus CPR/CPB cuticle proteins;
* absence of GAGAGS silk fibroin repeats, vitellogenin lipovitellin/VWD/DUF1943 architecture, Drosophila hexamerin/apolipophorin plasma signature.

C camp evidence:
* the same N-terminal signal followed downstream by GH-like blocks WWN TINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAPNILTPGHGQYLAVHTFLLSHARGLQTVRREFK and the C-terminal VIGYTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK motif present at the very 3′ end of the fragment;
* the in-frame TAA …CGCCTGTAA TGAAGG… interpreted as intronic, with the intron/exon organisation reported for Microcerotermes annadalai/annandalei GH genes and exon resumption ATG GAT GGC… after the intron, giving a ~330–500 aa secreted cellulolytic glycoside hydrolase with TIM-barrel/GH fold;
* nucleotide/aa identity claims to Microcerotermes annadalai glycoside hydrolase/cellulase.

Unresolved tension: the premature in-frame TAA and the subsequent YTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK motif at the 3′ end of the supplied fragment are hard to reconcile with a simple cDNA model. C supporters explain the stop as intronic and the downstream sequence as spliced GH exons; D supporters explain the downstream sequence as 3′ UTR and the stop as genuine termination of a small secreted cuticle protein.

With the exact 5′ nucleotide match to O. formosanus OfCP and the clean in-frame termination at ~250 aa, the parsimonious cDNA interpretation in the current round is a secreted structural cuticle protein, Rebers-Riddiford/CPR type, from Odontotermes formosanus, rather than a full-length Microcerotermes annadalai glycoside hydrolase. Silk fibroin, Drosophila plasma protein and Parnassius vitellogenin remain clearly excluded. Current leaning is D, Cuticle protein from Odontotermes formosanus, with the caveat that the intron/exon explanation for the GH interpretation remains the main counter-argument.

